# Task brief template — not an authorized task

Replace the example metadata and fill the sections with current task facts.
Do not add provider-specific model or thinking fields.

## Objective

Describe the user's requested observable outcome.

## Baseline and scope

Record the inspected revision, relevant existing behavior, exact allowed paths
and effects, and what must remain unchanged. Include applicable design or API
evidence for the actual task; do not require unrelated project artifacts.

## Execution agreement

Name the executor and freeze the review point, permitted in-scope repairs, expected
impact, evidence location, budget and genuine stop conditions. The executor
continues through implementation and scoped verification without requiring a new
user instruction after each test. Updates do not require approval. Preserve any
explicit audit-only/tests-only limit; amend earlier stop lines explicitly.

For fault-injection repairs: valid normal controls on old/candidate → identical
fault tests → actual entry chain → affected modules. Fixture failure is not an
old defect. Small changes use targeted checks; medium changes cover affected
consumers; refactors require full regression. Documentation-only changes use
structural checks. Two attempts without new evidence require a different diagnosis,
not blind reruns. Missing authority, exhausted budget or unavailable prerequisites
produce a precise blocker/checkpoint, never a false completion.

## Acceptance and verification

State success and negative cases, test scope, needed host/platform evidence and
how results will bind to the tested candidate. Record unavailable checks as
unverified rather than passed.

Identify old defects, candidate fixes, new regressions and invalid tests separately.
Bind code, tests, fixtures/configuration, commands and results to durable evidence.
Submit one consolidated independent-review handoff when scoped checks pass; do not
self-accept. Keep unrelated discoveries in follow-ups, not new acceptance gates.

## Recovery and open decisions

Define reversible recovery where possible. Route changes of scope and high-risk
effects through the current host's human decision surface. This template itself
does not transfer authority, settle effects or authorize publication.
